<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/syntax-trees-and-rules">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Morphology built words; syntax builds sentences — and it builds them
    <strong>hierarchically</strong>, not as beads on a string. The sentence you hear is a
    linear sequence of words, but the machinery that produced it is a tree: words grouped
    into phrases, phrases into larger phrases, the whole thing nested several layers deep.
    Two of linguistics' greatest discoveries follow directly: <strong>structural
    ambiguity</strong> (one string of words can correspond to two different trees) and
    <strong>displacement</strong> (words can end up far from where their meaning puts them).
    Grammar is the machinery nobody is taught and everybody acquires — this lesson makes it
    visible.
  </p>
  <p>
    The instrument is the <strong>phrase structure tree</strong>. It may look like
    bookkeeping, but it is a claim about cognition: that your mind stores sentences as
    nested units, groups "the old man" as one object, and computes "who did what to whom"
    from the geometry, not the word order. Every later topic — questions, relative
    clauses, constraints — is something the tree explains that the string cannot.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="trees, ambiguity, recursion, movement" widget="equationStep" config={syntaxCfg} />

  <h2>Constituency: finding the units</h2>
  <p>
    The core claim: words group into <strong>constituents</strong>, and a sentence is a
    labelled nesting of them. A minimal parse of "The dog chased the cat": a Noun Phrase
    [the dog], a Verb Phrase [chased [the cat]] whose object is another Noun Phrase, and
    the Sentence made of those two. The claims are testable, because constituents behave
    as units. <strong>Substitution</strong>: [the dog] can be replaced by "he" as a whole.
    <strong>Movement</strong>: [the cat] can front as one piece ("The cat, the dog chased").
    <strong>Coordination</strong>: [the dog] and [the cat] can join with "and" as units.
    <strong>The question test</strong>: to ask about [the cat] you move the whole thing
    ("What did the dog chase?"). Strings that pass these tests are constituents — and the
    tests, run on any sentence, reveal the hidden tree without any theory loaded in
    advance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Trees then do real explanatory work. <strong>Structural ambiguity</strong>: "I saw the
    man with the telescope" has two parses — the prepositional phrase attaches to "saw"
    (I used the telescope) or to "man" (he has it). Same string, different trees, different
    meanings; the ear flickers between them. Headlines mine this seam constantly ("Squad
    Helps Dog Bite Victim", "Two Sisters Reunited After 18 Years at Checkout Counter") —
    each is funny precisely because the structure, not any single word, carries the wrong
    meaning. Ambiguity that survives word-by-word paraphrase is the proof that structure
    is real.
  </p>

  <h2>Recursion: no ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    Phrase structure rules refer to themselves, and that is the deepest fact in the
    lesson. A Noun Phrase can contain a relative clause, which contains a sentence, which
    contains another Noun Phrase — "the cat [the dog [the man owned] chased] ran". English
    comprehension gives out after two nestings (that example is already at the edge), but
    that is memory, not grammar: the rule system itself places no ceiling on depth. Recursion
    is what makes language <strong>productive</strong> — a finite inventory of words and
    rules yielding sentences nobody has ever said before, indefinitely many of them. Every
    new sentence you produce today is a demonstration.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same machinery explains dependencies at distance. In "The key to the cabinets is
    on the table", the verb agrees with "key", not the nearer "cabinets" — agreement tracks
    the structural head, not the linear neighbour, which is exactly what a tree encodes
    and a string does not. Children acquiring English make agreement errors with local
    nouns (the experimental literature on "agreement attraction") — evidence that even
    immature grammars are computing structure, not strings.
  </p>

  <h2>Movement: where words are not where they come from</h2>
  <p>
    Questions expose the tree even more sharply. "What did you eat?" — "what" is the
    <em>object</em> of eat (compare "You ate what"), but it sits at the front of the
    sentence, leaving a gap after the verb. The standard analysis: the question word
    <strong>moves</strong> from its argument position to the front, and the grammar
    simultaneously tracks the launch site and the landing site. Passives work the same
    way ("The cat was chased" — the object became the subject); relative clauses
    ("the cat [that the dog chased __]") and even whole-clause questions inherit the
    machinery.
  </p>
  <p>
    The expert payoff is that movement is <strong>constrained</strong>. Try to question out
    of an embedded clause with a question of its own — "What do you wonder whether Mary
    ate?" — and the sentence collapses: elements cannot cross certain structural barriers
    (the "island" constraints). No teacher shows you these constraints; every speaker
    respects them without knowing they exist. Universal and unlearned — this is the
    evidence pattern that launched the generative programme, and the reason syntax became
    linguistics' most theory-driven branch.
  </p>

  <h2>Verbs and their appetites: subcategorisation</h2>
  <p>
    Verbs determine what can follow them, and the trees record the demands. "Devour"
    requires an object ("The dragon devoured the village", never "The dragon devoured");
    "arrive" refuses one; "put" demands both an object and a location ("put it there");
    "eat" is flexible — it can drop its object when the meal is understood. The classic
    tacit-knowledge pair: "The cat seems sleeping" is out, but "The cat seems to be
    sleeping" is fine, and "seems" cannot take a simple object at all ("We seem it" is
    nonsense). Every speaker commands thousands of these verb-specific requirements —
    again, without instruction. In the trees, these appetites appear as frames: which
    arguments a verb selects, and where they sit. This is also where syntax and meaning
    meet — verbs assign roles (who does, who undergoes) to their structural positions, the
    bridge the semantics lesson will cross.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "grammar" as taught in school (don't split
  infinitives, no sentence fragments) is prescriptive etiquette. Descriptive syntax is
  something different and deeper: the rules every speaker actually obeys — the islands,
  the subcategorisation frames, the constituency tests. Both exist; only one is a
  science.</p>

  <h2>Typology: the same machine, different settings</h2>
  <p>
    Word order varies more than the trees do. Languages cluster by basic order — SVO
    (English, Mandarin), SOV (Japanese, Turkish, Korean), VSO (Irish, Arabic) — and the
    orders correlate: SOV languages tend to place modifiers before heads (postpositions,
    relative clauses before nouns); SVO languages after. But the deeper structures
    translate: Japanese "not" negates the same layer of the clause English does, and every
    known language has subjects and predicates, questions, and relative clauses built by
    the same toolkit. Whether the universal design lives in the principles or in the
    communicative problems all languages solve is the live debate — but the correlations
    (Greenberg's, from the 1960s, confirmed by ever-larger samples) are stable facts.
  </p>

  <h2>A full parse, step by step</h2>
  <p>
    Assemble the toolkit on one sentence: "The old dog slept under the table." First find
    the units with the tests. [the old dog] passes substitution ("He slept…"), fronting
    ("The old dog, under the table, slept" — awkward but grammatical) and the question
    test ("Which dog slept?" — the whole phrase moves). [under the table] passes too:
    substitution ("slept there"), fronting ("Under the table, the old dog slept"),
    coordination ("under the table and behind the sofa"). So the tree: Sentence →
    Noun Phrase + Verb Phrase; the Noun Phrase → determiner + adjective + noun; the
    Verb Phrase → verb + Prepositional Phrase; the Prepositional Phrase → preposition +
    Noun Phrase. The nesting is three deep, and every bracket is a claim about grouping
    that the tests just verified. This two-pass procedure — test, then label — is all
    tree-drawing is, and it works identically on German, Arabic or Warlpiri sentences
    with the word order rearranged: the tree, not the order, is the invariant.
  </p>

  <h2>Do-support: a fossil that proves the tree</h2>
  <p>
    One quirk of English supplies unusually clean evidence. Questions and negation need a
    slot that ordinary verbs cannot fill: "You ate" becomes "Did you eat?" and "You did
    not eat" — a "do" appears from nowhere, and the main verb drops its tense ("eat", not
    "ate"). Why? Because the question and negation target a structural position (call it
    the tense head) that lexical verbs cannot occupy in modern English; only the small
    class of auxiliaries (be, have, modals) can. "Do" is inserted purely to carry the
    tense — <strong>do-support</strong> — and its behaviour is ruthlessly systematic:
    "You ate what" → "What did you eat?", never "What you ate?" with the verb raised,
    even though French does exactly that ("Qu'as-tu mangé?"). English and French differ
    in one setting — whether the verb may move to the tense head — and every sentence of
    each language respects it. A grammatical fossil (do-support is centuries old) thus
    still displays the machinery: the tree's nodes are real positions, and languages
    differ in what may occupy them.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions across languages: one computation, two strategies</h2>
  <p>
    Movement is not the only way to build a question, and the alternatives confirm what
    the machinery is. Mandarin leaves question words where the missing argument belongs —
    "nǐ chī shénme" is literally "you eat what" — a strategy called <strong>wh-in-situ</strong>;
    Japanese does the same (and marks questions with a sentence-final particle). English
    instead moves the question word to the front. But the two grammars compute the same
    information: in both, "what" must be understood as the object of "eat", and both
    respect locality the same way. Even more striking: children acquiring Mandarin and
    English make parallel developmental steps on their different schedules, and the
    movement strategy leaves traces English speakers can hear — the ungrammaticality of
    "What did you wonder whether Mary ate?" only exists in languages that move at all.
    Comparing the strategies is the cleanest demonstration that the tree (with its
    argument positions) is the deep object and the linear order a variable surface
    convention.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers of expert detail. <strong>Formal strength</strong>: phrase structure
    grammars sit in the Chomsky hierarchy as (mildly) context-free systems, and the
    "mildly" matters — natural language needs slightly more than pure context-free power
    (copying constructions like "The more you eat, the fatter you get"), which is one
    reason generative grammar developed movement traces and, later, minimalist operations
    (merge, agree) that generate the tree by combining items pairwise. <strong>Acquisition
    evidence</strong>: children produce rule-shaped errors ("goed", "more taller"), respect
    island constraints by the preschool years, and correct without overt correction — the
    "poverty of the stimulus" argument holds that the input underdetermines the grammar
    they attain; the counterargument (richer input statistics, usage-based learning) is
    the field's central controversy, still live. <strong>Computation</strong>: parsing
    (recovering the tree from the string) is what a grammar checker, a compiler front-end
    for programming languages, and a machine translator all do; humans do it incrementally
    and fall into "garden paths" — "The horse raced past the barn fell" reads fine once
    you find the reduced-relative parse — and modern language models, trained on text
    alone, develop internal representations measurably correlated with syntactic trees,
    the newest piece of evidence in a 60-year argument about whether structure is
    learned or assumed. The trees, it turns out, can be measured in machines as well
    as in minds.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chomsky, Syntactic Structures (1957); Carnie, Syntax: A Generative Introduction; Huddleston &amp; Pullum, The Cambridge Grammar of the English Language.</p>
